d 'CDID': module to perform chained difference-in-differences for complete and incomplete panels
d
d      cdid estimates group-time average treatment effects on the
d treated, ATT(g,t): the average effect at date t on the units
d first    treated at date g, in designs where units adopt the
d treatment at different dates and remain treated. It implements
d the chained	 difference-in-differences estimator of Bellégo,
d Benatia, and Dortet-Bernadet (2025) within the framework of
d Callaway and	  Sant'Anna (2021).    In chained mode, the
d default, each unit contributes the changes of its outcome between
d the dates at which it is observed, so    the panel need not be
d complete.  These comparisons are combined by the generalized
d method of moments (GMM). The targets remain	 the effects on the
d cohorts of the study population, including units whose outcomes
d are missing at some dates (except that,    under the default
d missingreference(exclude), a cohort whose treated units lack a
d required reference covariate is estimated for    its eligible
d treated units); the assumptions concern the missing changes
d rather than the missing levels. An effect that the	observed
d comparisons cannot recover is reported as unavailable, with a
d reason, and is never set to zero. 
d
d KW: difference in differences
d KW: DiD
d KW: chained
d KW: ATE
d
d Requires: Stata version 19.5 and csdid from SSC (q.v.)
d
d Distribution-Date: 20261125
d
d Author: David Benatia,  HEC Montréal
d Support: email david.benatia@@hec.ca
d
